<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ribchester</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bremetennacum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ribchester is a village on the River Ribble in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the site of the Roman fort of Bremetennacum. From the later second century it housed a cavalry unit of Sarmatians, descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a> sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> in 175 CE and still recorded there in the third century. The fort has yielded the Ribchester Helmet, a decorated Roman cavalry helmet now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ribchester.jpg" alt="Ribchester" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Watcher328 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Ribchester.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ribchester is a village and civil parish in the Ribble Valley district, six miles north-west of Blackburn and twelve miles east of Preston. It stands at the foot of Longridge Fell on boulder clay laid by the River Ribble, whose meanders have left terraces around the village; the river is prone to spates and often floods it in winter. Stonygate Lane, running north, partly follows the old Roman road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations at Parsonage Avenue found a circular ditch of about 1300 BCE with urns holding cremated remains. The first Roman fort was built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> in 72 or 73 CE by the 20th legion, renovated late in the 1st century and rebuilt in stone early in the 2nd, and a fort remained until the 4th century. Covering about 6 acres and garrisoned by cavalry, it was a large fort; work by the Manchester Classical Association in 1913 traced its headquarters building, which unusually fronted the main street with a colonnade. The Ribchester Hoard, found in 1796 about 10 feet below the surface, held the helmet with paterae, two basins and a bust of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>.</p><p>St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a>'s Church, first recorded in 1193 and believed to have been founded in the 8th century, stands almost over the fort's headquarters. John Leland in the 1540s described great squared stones, vaults and ancient coins, and William Camden recorded the saying that Ribchester was as rich as any town in Christendom. In the 17th and 18th centuries the village became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> weaving, first in the weavers' homes and later in Bee Mill and Corporation Mill; the last weaving business closed in the 1980s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The village had 1,598 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and is chiefly a home for commuters to Blackburn, Preston and Manchester. The excavated granaries beside the churchyard and the Roman baths can be seen, and the Ribchester Roman Museum, founded by Margaret Greenall in 1915, holds many finds from the fort, with a replica of the helmet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ribchester” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome recruited Sarmatian horsemen, most famously the 5,500 Iazyges sent to Britain in 175 CE, and an inscription from <b>Ribchester</b> in Lancashire records a unit of Sarmatian cavalry still stationed there in the third century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
